Fix a data race on the host cursor surface in the nested Wayland backend.

SetCursorImage() destroys and recreates m_pCursorSurface on the compositor
thread, while UpdateCursor() is also invoked from the Wayland input thread's
callbacks (pointer enter, keyboard enter/leave) and passes the very same
pointer to wl_pointer.set_cursor. When a click lands while the client changes
its cursor image, a request naming an already destroyed wl_surface goes out,
the host compositor raises a protocol error ("error in client communication"
in niri) and CWaylandInputThread::ThreadFunc abort()s on the dead connection.

Reproduces within seconds with anything that swaps cursors under the pointer:
InstallShield installers (hourglass), Delphi autorun menus (hand cursor).
Still present upstream in 3.16.28.

--- a/src/Backends/WaylandBackend.cpp	2026-09-19 15:31:51.905771758 +0200
+++ b/src/Backends/WaylandBackend.cpp	2026-09-19 15:31:51.951045806 +0200
@@ -847,6 +847,10 @@
         wl_surface *m_pCursorSurface = nullptr;
         std::shared_ptr<INestedHints::CursorInfo> m_pDefaultCursorInfo;
         wl_surface *m_pDefaultCursorSurface = nullptr;
+
+        // Guards the cursor state above: SetCursorImage() runs on the compositor
+        // thread, UpdateCursor() also runs from the Wayland input thread.
+        std::recursive_mutex m_CursorLock;
     };
     const wl_registry_listener CWaylandBackend::s_RegistryListener =
     {
@@ -2396,6 +2400,8 @@

     void CWaylandBackend::SetCursorImage( std::shared_ptr<INestedHints::CursorInfo> info )
     {
+        std::scoped_lock lock{ m_CursorLock };
+
         m_pCursorInfo = info;

         if ( m_pCursorSurface )
